<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simón de Anda y Salazar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1709">1709</a>–<a href="/years/1776">1776</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simón de Anda y Salazar was a Spanish judge and colonial governor in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. When the British took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> in 1762 he escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pampanga">Pampanga</a>, declared himself governor and organised resistance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacolor">Bacolor</a> until the British left in 1764. He later served as governor-general of the Philippines from 1770 until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simon-de-anda-y-salazar.jpg" alt="Simón de Anda y Salazar" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author (currently held at the collection of Bernardo Estornés Lasa) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sim%C3%B3n_de_Anda_y_Salazar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Simón de Anda y Salazar was born on 28 October 1709 in the Basque Country of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, near the town of Subijana. He served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> as an oidor, a judge, of the Royal Audience of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>. During the British attack on the city, the Governor-General and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> appointed him Lieutenant Governor, and on the night of 5 October 1762, during the Battle of Manila, he left the city with much of the treasury and its documents.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the acting governor, Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-rojo-del-rio-y-vieyra">Manuel Rojo</a>, was captured by the British and surrendered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, Anda rejected the act as illegal. He assumed full authority on behalf of the Real Audiencia, set up a provisional government and raised an army, and kept up the campaign against the British. He returned unopened every letter that did not address him as Governor-General of the Philippines, which the British refused to do until Rojo's death on 30 January 1764, so early negotiations failed.</p><p>In March 1764 Brigadier Francisco de la Torre brought orders from both kings to hand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763, and on 1 April 1764 the British garrison marched out. Anda then travelled to Spain, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> received him well and the king made him a Councilor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, although he had written to the king complaining of disorders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, many of them laid at the door of the friars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Anda returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> on 12 April 1768 and by royal decree became Governor-General in July 1770. He proceeded against his predecessor and other officials, reformed the Spanish and Philippine army and undertook public works. He opposed the king's order of 9 November 1774 to secularise the curacies held by the regular clergy, which was repealed after his death. He died on 30 October 1776 in the Hospital de San Felipe in Cavite, aged 67. Monuments to him stand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacolor">Bacolor</a>, and the municipalities of Anda in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohol">Bohol</a> and Pangasinan bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Simón de Anda y Salazar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dawsonne Drake of the East India Company governed as deputy, while <b>Simón de Anda y Salazar</b> organised Spanish resistance from Bacolor in Pampanga.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-occupation-of-manila">British occupation of Manila</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Subijana-Morillas, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Cavite, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.82,-2.9&amp;year=1752" aria-label="Subijana-Morillas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4480 642 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Simón de Anda y Salazar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4930 912h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4930 912h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.82,-2.9&amp;year=1752">What was here in 1752? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
